\section{Raw-response plots}{

\code{\link[=count_wells_by_dose]{count_wells_by_dose()}} counts kept and total wells per dose group on the
full data. After removing \code{keep_well = FALSE} wells,
\code{\link[=plot_raw_responses_pages]{plot_raw_responses_pages()}} draws, per plate, the median trace of each
dose group or the traces of single wells (\code{\link[=plot_raw_responses]{plot_raw_responses()}} for one
plate, \code{\link[=summarize_raw_responses]{summarize_raw_responses()}} for the numbers).
}

\section{Inter-plate vehicle-control QC}{

Following Truong et al. (2026, Section 2.6.2.3), plates whose VC movement
pattern is unusual compared with the other plates are flagged:
\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=calculate_plate_reference_pattern]{calculate_plate_reference_pattern()}}: per plate, the median VC
response at each timepoint (the reference pattern).
\item \code{\link[=fit_plate_pca]{fit_plate_pca()}}: PCA of the plates' reference patterns.
\item \code{\link[=detect_plate_pca_outliers]{detect_plate_pca_outliers()}}: plates outside the 95\% confidence
ellipse of the first two components.
\item \code{\link[=plot_plate_pca_ellipse]{plot_plate_pca_ellipse()}} and \code{\link[=plot_plate_reference_overlay]{plot_plate_reference_overlay()}}
show the result; a person decides which flagged plates to exclude.
}
The within-plate VC check is Module 3 (\code{intraplate-vc-qc}, see
\link{module3}).
}

